<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Port Louis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Port Louis is the capital and main harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>. It was developed from 1735 by the French governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahe-de-la-bourdonnais">Mahé de La Bourdonnais</a> as the capital and naval base of Isle de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After the British conquest of 1810 it remained the seat of government of British Mauritius and the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/port-louis.jpg" alt="Port Louis" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thierry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Port_Louis_Skyline.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers used the site as a harbour from 1606 and called the area the Harbour of Tortoises. Under French government it became the administrative centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, then called Ile de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and a major reprovisioning halt for French ships passing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. The port is named in honour of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, and the French governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahe-de-la-bourdonnais">Bertrand-François Mahé de La Bourdonnais</a> contributed to the development of the city. Because the Moka Mountain Range shelters it from cyclone winds, it was chosen to house both the main harbour and the fort.</p><p>The port kept its value during the British occupation of the island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, when it helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. Port calls fell drastically after the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 1869, but rose during the canal's seven-year closure from 1967. Modernisation in the late 1970s helped it remain the central point for all imports and exports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Port Louis is the capital and most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, with 147,066 inhabitants at the 2018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The main harbour is the only official port of entry and exit for sea vessels in the country, and ships must be cleared there before visiting any other anchorage. It is the biggest port facility in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> region, with specialised terminals for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, fish and containers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Many buildings reflect the city's colonial history, among them French colonial buildings of the 18th century such as Government House, the Jummah Mosque, Saint James Cathedral, a Tamil temple and the five-tier Port Louis Theatre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aapravasi-ghat">Aapravasi Ghat</a> is the remains of the immigration depot built by the British government to import labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Eastern Africa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> to work on the island's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> estates. The Intercontinental Slavery Museum deals with slavery and indentured labour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Port Louis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>HMS Clio under Captain James Onslow arrived at the Falklands and required the small Argentine garrison at <b>Port Louis</b> to leave.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-3">3 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The island was ceded in the 1814 Treaty of Paris, renamed Mauritius, and its first governor, Robert Farquhar, ruled from <b>Port Louis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-mauritius">British Mauritius</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers arrived from Ile Bourbon in 1721, and under governor Mahe de La Bourdonnais from 1735 the colony was developed, with <b>Port Louis</b> built as a capital and naval harbour.</blockquote><a href="/state/isle-de-france">Isle de France</a></li></ul></section>
